@c ======================================================================
@c  01-domain-analysis.weft — GENERAL DOMAIN ANALYSIS (step 02).
@c  The concepts, entities and invariants the system turns on, plus the
@c  vocabulary the whole book will speak. Prose-only: NO @o, NO @d.
@c ======================================================================

\chapter{Domain analysis}

\section{Concepts}

<The concepts the domain decomposes into. One concept = one chapter later =
one narrative crossing every layer and language. Name each one and say what it
OWNS — ownership is what makes a boundary real.>

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{<concept-a>} --- <what it owns>
  \item \textbf{<concept-b>} --- <what it owns>
\end{itemize}

\section{Entities}

<The things that have identity and state: what they are, what they hold, and
which concept is responsible for them.>

\section{Invariants}

<The laws that must always hold. Hunt for the one that DEFINES the domain: the
rule whose violation means you no longer have the thing at all (a ledger that
can go unbalanced is not a ledger with a bug --- it is not a ledger). For each
invariant, say WHERE it is enforced and WHY there.>

\begin{description}
  \item[<invariant>] <statement>. Enforced in <concept>, because <why there>.
\end{description}

\section{Vocabulary}

<Every core term the rest of the book will use. These become the glossary
entries and the DOMAIN prefixes of chunk names (term ``posting'' → chunks named
``Ledger: post entry''). Every domain word a chunk name later uses MUST trace
back to a definition here --- that is what keeps one language across the book.>

\begin{description}
  \item[<term>] <definition>.
  \item[<term>] <definition>.
\end{description}
